---
import { GITHUB_URL } from '../consts';
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { url } from '../url';

const LINKEDIN_URL = 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/brian-fotheringham';
---

<Page
	title="About"
	description="Brian Fotheringham: data and AI platform architect at GE Aerospace, working on governed data for people and agents."
>
	<article class="wrap">
		<header class="head">
			<p class="eyebrow">About</p>
			<h1>About</h1>
		</header>
		<div class="prose">
			<p>
				I'm Brian Fotheringham. I'm a data and AI platform architect at GE Aerospace, based in
				Cleveland. I work on the part of the stack where data meets AI: getting data to people and
				agents quickly, while keeping clear who owns it, who can use it, and why.
			</p>
			<p>
				My working style is simple. Set a standard, enforce it with tooling instead of memory, roll it
				out crawl-walk-run, and change it when reality pushes back. I like cloud primitives, small
				versioned config files, and systems that fail closed.
			</p>

			<h2>Work</h2>
			<p>
				I'm a data and AI architect at GE Aerospace, where I've worked since 2020 across data
				engineering, data platforms, and architecture. Most of my work now is technical direction:
				setting architecture and standards for data and AI platforms in a regulated environment, and
				leading the teams that build them.
			</p>
			<p>The kinds of things I've built and used:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Lakehouses on open table formats.</strong> Apache Iceberg on S3 with Lake Formation
					and Glue, designed so several platforms read one copy of the data instead of each making
					their own. Earlier, large data lakes on Spark, Glue, EMR and Apache Hudi.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Governed analytics platforms.</strong> Databricks and Unity Catalog organized by data
					domain, so each domain owns its data, with catalog federation so data stays usable across
					platforms.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Ingestion on cloud primitives.</strong> Config-driven, self-service ingestion built on
					AWS ECS and Batch, handling structured and unstructured sources, in place of a licensed
					product.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Data contracts.</strong> Contracts on the Open Data Contract Standard that declare a
					dataset's owner, schema, SLAs, quality checks and classification, enforced in CI/CD.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Attribute-based access control.</strong> Policy frameworks where access follows
					written policy and data attributes instead of one-off grants, with identity from Microsoft
					Entra ID.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>MCP servers and gateways for agents.</strong> A shared MCP package with identity,
					policy enforcement, audit and observability built in, plus security review standards for
					MCP servers.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Observability for agent tooling.</strong> OpenTelemetry streamed through Kinesis
					Firehose into Iceberg and queried with DuckDB.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Agent platforms.</strong> Publishing and running agents on AWS Bedrock AgentCore and
					Strands, with ownership, audit and change management built in.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>Open source and writing</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<a href="https://github.com/FatherBrian/trellis"><strong>Trellis and Reef</strong></a>:
					open-source tools that let an AI agent act for a person only within a task's scope. Every
					tool call passes Cedar policy checks and lands in a hash-chained audit ledger.
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href={url('/blog/primitives-for-agents/')}><strong>Primitives for agents</strong></a>:
					why agents need small, versioned, tool-enforced config files, and the first one,
					<code>reef.lock</code>, which pins MCP tool definitions so a changed tool can't slip through.
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href={url('/lab/')}><strong>The Lab</strong></a>: live demos of the ideas I write about.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>Education</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>M.S. Computer Science, Artificial Intelligence, Georgia Institute of Technology (2026)</li>
				<li>B.S. Software Engineering and B.S. Information Systems, Miami University (2020)</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>Elsewhere</h2>
			<p>
				<a href={GITHUB_URL}>GitHub</a> · <a href={LINKEDIN_URL}>LinkedIn</a> ·
				<a href={url('/rss.xml')}>RSS</a>
			</p>
		</div>
	</article>
</Page>

<style>
	.head {
		max-width: var(--measure);
		margin: 4.5rem auto 2rem;
	}
	.head .eyebrow {
		display: none;
	}
	@media (max-width: 640px) {
		.head {
			margin-top: 2.75rem;
		}
	}
</style>
